WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.445 --> 00:00:05.379
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:17.110
I was an undergraduate at
MIT in the mid to late '60s.

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:20.110
There were very few
African-American students.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:22.570
I really wanted to
make a difference

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:28.570
and have MIT be more hospitable
to minority students.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:31.540
We created a summer program
called Project Interphase,

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.640
which still operates.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.430
I was in the first
Project Interphase,

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:39.310
which took place in 1969.

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:44.050
It was the singular most
important academic experience

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:46.420
I ever had in my life.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:49.150
Interphase invites
incoming freshmen

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:52.090
to spend a summer at
MIT, gain community,

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.890
get to know the
campus, and also have

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:58.360
a taste of the coursework to
get a jump on the new semester.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.030
If you do Interphase, you
hit the ground running.

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:05.200
It's not for the credits that
you get, but for those skills

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.360
that you learn in those
classes, especially study

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.370
skills and office hours.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:11.020
Oh, jeez, office hours.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.770
No one knew about
them coming in.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:16.060
But office hours
are so important.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.070
It puts you ahead,
and it'll let you

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.720
know what you're going to
see during the semester.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.170
And it really helped
me in that transition.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:27.350
And to this day, my Interphase
cohort are my closest friends.

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:28.960
So they're the people
I hang out with,

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.410
the people I do homework
with, I study with.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:34.720
I recall that
experience being really

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.880
instrumental in
helping me to feel

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.640
a part of the MIT community.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.460
We invite departments,
labs, and centers,

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.370
and student facing
organizations to come and talk

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:48.070
to the Interphasers about
all of their resources.

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:51.250
How to get support from
Student Support Services,

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.770
or how to go to Career Advising
and Professional Development

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:55.930
and work on a resume
and actually interview

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:57.790
for job opportunities.

00:01:57.790 --> 00:01:59.860
And if they are interested
in certain majors,

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:01.475
we have department
administrators

00:02:01.475 --> 00:02:03.850
who are there to talk to them
about different disciplines

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:08.440
and why these majors might
be exciting for them.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.255
Interphase was a nice nest.

00:02:10.255 --> 00:02:12.630
And it's what I want to feel
like for the rest of my four

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:14.120
years.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.520
Students who come
to MIT realize,

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:23.060
when they get here that, they
themselves are very talented,

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:24.470
but so is everyone else.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:26.960
And that introduces
a need for them

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:31.610
to learn how to both give
help and receive help.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:34.258
MIT is so much harder
than you think it is.

00:02:34.258 --> 00:02:36.800
You have to learn to think about
the world in different ways.

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:38.800
Think about problems
differently.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.400
Really figure out how you
learn, how to ask for help.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.720
And that is something you just
don't get as a high schooler.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.030
It's more than just being
invited to the number one

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.150
engineering school and having
access to all these professors,

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:54.850
but it's being taught
how to navigate it.

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:57.280
It's being comfortable
in your own skin.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:00.520
It's feeling like you
actually belong here.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.690
It's definitely a community
that you stick with.

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:04.877
It's not just something
that happens in the summer

00:03:04.877 --> 00:03:06.460
and then you go off
into your classes.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.770
And then you say
hi in the hallways.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:12.110
It's a security net so that
you can feel comfortable.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:15.100
It's a hammock.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.590
It's wonderful for me to be
back here as a faculty member

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:19.660
and think about
28 years ago first

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:22.000
being introduced
to the OME and how

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.830
profound an impact
those programs

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:26.440
had on myself as an
individual, as well

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:28.900
as on my professional career.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:31.472
I don't know where I would
be without Interphase.

00:03:31.472 --> 00:03:32.680
Thank god I did this program.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

